{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788182879,"nonce":"GufQpJ/EQLYYwDRlRp8axYaREyk=","title":"3.4: manual work priorities cannot be turned on by any verb, so work-priorities is unreachable on a fresh colony","message":"Found during 3.4's acceptance on the Linux bench, 2026-08-31.\n\n`work-priorities` correctly refuses priority 1 or 2 when the colony has manual\npriorities off, with the game's own reasoning:\n\n  \"priority 2 is meaningless with manual priorities off: the Work tab is a\n   checkbox then and GetPriority returns a flat 3. Use 0 or 3, or turn manual\n   priorities on in the play settings.\"\n\nThat refusal is right — it is the gate-in-the-widget doctrine working, and\nPawn_WorkSettings.SetPriority would otherwise write state the Work tab cannot\nshow. The problem is the second half: manual priorities default OFF on a new\ncolony, and NOTHING in the shipped verb surface can turn them on. The flag is\n`Current.Game.playSettings.useWorkPriorities`, which is a play setting, not a\npawn field, and no observer or actor touches it.\n\nConsequence: 8 of 3.4's acceptance checks (4.7a-e, 5.1a-c) are unreachable on\nany fixture built from a fresh map, because they use priorities 1 and 2. The\nspec's own acceptance therefore cannot pass headlessly on a colony the agent\nstaged itself — only on a save where a human had already ticked the box.\n\nAcceptance\n\n- A player verb can turn manual work priorities on and off, citing the widget\n  that owns the toggle (the Work tab's checkbox / PlaySettings), and reports the\n  before/after value.\n- OR 3.4's acceptance is amended to enable it first, and the enabling route is\n  named. Whichever is chosen, the answer is recorded in DESIGN's decisions log\n  rather than left to the next fixture builder to rediscover.\n- `work-priorities` keeps its current refusal text either way; it is good and it\n  is what surfaced this.","files":null}]}